\"mjx-c29\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo></mjx-TeXAtom></mjx-script></mjx-msup></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mrow data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">d</mi></mrow><mrow data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container></span> is the\nNewton direction. Why does the undamped step not carry the same\nguarantee? (Compare <span class=\"citation\" data-cites=\"prp-res-irls\">@prp-res-irls</span>, where the undamped step\n<em>is</em> monotone, because the argument there is majorization rather\nthan Newton.)</p>","file":"ch34-exponential-families-glm/04-irls.html"}